WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.932
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.232 --> 00:00:08.526
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.826 --> 00:00:16.192
Unit twenty-seven: Mastering Public Speaking. Today you will build memorable rhythm with tricolon and parallelism.

4
00:00:16.492 --> 00:00:19.214
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:23.434
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.283 --> 00:00:25.575
Public speaking.

7
00:00:26.583 --> 00:00:29.930
Public speaking means the act of speaking to a live audience.

8
00:00:30.517 --> 00:00:33.550
Public speaking terrifies more people than heights.

9
00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:36.930
Your turn. Say: Public speaking.

10
00:00:39.767 --> 00:00:40.861
Public speaking.

11
00:00:41.617 --> 00:00:42.571
Delivery.

12
00:00:43.583 --> 00:00:46.709
Delivery means the manner in which a speech is presented.

13
00:00:47.317 --> 00:00:50.024
Her delivery was calm, warm and deliberate.

14
00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:53.733
Your turn. Say: Delivery.

15
00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:57.694
Delivery.

16
00:00:58.450 --> 00:00:59.312
Pause.

17
00:01:00.317 --> 00:01:03.013
Pause means a deliberate moment of silence.

18
00:01:03.617 --> 00:01:06.580
A well-timed pause gives an idea time to land.

19
00:01:07.183 --> 00:01:09.662
Your turn. Say: Pause.

20
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:13.327
Pause.

21
00:01:14.083 --> 00:01:15.131
Storytelling.

22
00:01:16.117 --> 00:01:19.289
Storytelling means using narrative to convey ideas.

23
00:01:19.883 --> 00:01:22.614
Storytelling made the statistics unforgettable.

24
00:01:23.217 --> 00:01:25.985
Your turn. Say: Storytelling.

25
00:01:28.833 --> 00:01:29.881
Storytelling.

26
00:01:30.617 --> 00:01:31.525
Presence.

27
00:01:32.517 --> 00:01:36.107
Presence means the quality of commanding attention through composure.

28
00:01:36.717 --> 00:01:39.970
Stage presence comes from preparation, not perfection.

29
00:01:40.583 --> 00:01:43.247
Your turn. Say: Presence.

30
00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:47.148
Presence.

31
00:01:47.883 --> 00:01:48.873
Audience.

32
00:01:49.883 --> 00:01:52.638
Audience means the people listening to a speech.

33
00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:55.981
Know your audience before you write a single word.

34
00:01:56.583 --> 00:01:59.120
Your turn. Say: Audience.

35
00:02:01.967 --> 00:02:03.037
Audience.

36
00:02:03.783 --> 00:02:04.715
Structure.

37
00:02:05.717 --> 00:02:08.900
Structure means the organisation of a speech's parts.

38
00:02:09.517 --> 00:02:13.130
A clear structure helps listeners follow complex ideas.

39
00:02:13.717 --> 00:02:16.532
Your turn. Say: Structure.

40
00:02:19.367 --> 00:02:20.321
Structure.

41
00:02:21.083 --> 00:02:22.189
Confidence.

42
00:02:23.183 --> 00:02:25.717
Confidence means belief in one's ability.

43
00:02:26.317 --> 00:02:28.595
Confidence grows with every rehearsal.

44
00:02:29.183 --> 00:02:31.743
Your turn. Say: Confidence.

45
00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:35.706
Confidence.

46
00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:40.348
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:41.183 --> 00:02:46.100
Visal, I have to give a graduation speech next month. How can I make it memorable?

48
00:02:47.183 --> 00:02:57.269
Use rhythm. Great speakers often group ideas in threes: 'We came, we listened, we changed.' The pattern feels complete and is easy to remember.

49
00:02:58.383 --> 00:03:00.069
Isn't that a bit theatrical?

50
00:03:01.183 --> 00:03:12.114
Only if overused. One or two well-placed tricolons, supported by a personal story and a deliberate pause, will give your speech shape and energy.

51
00:03:13.217 --> 00:03:14.624
And if I get nervous?

52
00:03:15.717 --> 00:03:22.850
Prepare, rehearse, breathe. Nervousness means you care; structure means you won't get lost.

53
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.419
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:26.283 --> 00:03:29.235
Grammar focus: Rhetorical devices (tricolon).

55
00:03:29.535 --> 00:03:30.838
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:33.770
We came, we listened, we changed.

57
00:03:34.683 --> 00:03:36.323
Prepare, rehearse, breathe.

58
00:03:37.217 --> 00:03:39.762
Government of the people, by the people, for the people.

59
00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:51.386
A tricolon is a series of three parallel words, phrases or clauses. Keep the grammar identical in each part (all verbs, all clauses) and place the strongest idea last for a sense of climax.

60
00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:55.754
Be careful! Don't say: We came, we listened, and change.

61
00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.048
Say: We came, we listened, we changed.

62
00:04:00.348 --> 00:04:01.999
Keep a tricolon parallel.

63
00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:05.603
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:07.691
The Power of Three.

65
00:04:07.991 --> 00:04:14.101
Since antiquity, orators have recognised that ideas grouped in threes are especially persuasive and memorable.

66
00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:22.591
Julius Caesar's 'I came, I saw, I conquered' and Lincoln's 'of the people, by the people, for the people' remain famous partly because of this rhythm.

67
00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:28.908
The tricolon works because three items create a pattern while remaining short enough for listeners to retain.

68
00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:36.416
Yet rhetorical devices succeed only when supported by authentic content, clear structure and confident delivery.

69
00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:45.669
Modern research on public speaking also highlights the power of storytelling and strategic pauses, which give audiences time to absorb and feel each idea.

70
00:04:46.767 --> 00:04:51.082
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:51.917 --> 00:04:54.009
Which sentence is a correct tricolon?

72
00:04:54.309 --> 00:05:01.132
A, We learned, we grew, and succeeding.. B, We learned, we grew, we succeeded.. C, We learn, grew, success..

73
00:05:04.667 --> 00:05:07.618
The answer is B: We learned, we grew, we succeeded..

74
00:05:08.783 --> 00:05:12.048
The manner in which a speech is presented is its blank.

75
00:05:12.348 --> 00:05:16.071
A, delivery. B, audience. C, structure.

76
00:05:19.633 --> 00:05:21.552
The answer is A: delivery.

77
00:05:22.717 --> 00:05:25.239
A deliberate moment of silence is a blank.

78
00:05:25.539 --> 00:05:29.424
A, presence. B, storytelling. C, pause.

79
00:05:32.967 --> 00:05:34.850
The answer is C: pause.

80
00:05:36.017 --> 00:05:38.109
Choose the most parallel sentence.

81
00:05:38.409 --> 00:05:46.614
A, She is smart, kind, and works hard.. B, She is smart, kind and hard-working.. C, She is smart, kindness and hard work..

82
00:05:50.167 --> 00:05:53.768
The answer is B: She is smart, kind and hard-working..

83
00:05:54.917 --> 00:05:58.600
According to the reading, the tricolon works because three items…

84
00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:04.480
A, are short and form a pattern. B, are always funny. C, confuse the audience.

85
00:06:08.033 --> 00:06:11.159
The answer is A: are short and form a pattern.

86
00:06:12.367 --> 00:06:15.483
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:18.570
What techniques make a speaker memorable?

88
00:06:35.383 --> 00:06:36.884
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:37.184 --> 00:07:11.144
A memorable speaker combines clear structure, vivid stories and confident delivery. Structure helps listeners follow the argument, while personal stories make abstract ideas concrete and emotional. Rhetorical devices such as the tricolon give key messages rhythm, making them easier to remember. Equally important is delivery: eye contact, varied pace and deliberate pauses show confidence and let ideas land. In short, the best speakers prepare thoroughly, speak sincerely and connect personally.

90
00:07:11.444 --> 00:07:13.328
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:14.217 --> 00:07:18.703
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and rhetorical devices (tricolon).

92
00:07:19.003 --> 00:07:24.117
Today's value is eloquence. Choose your words with care, and speak to persuade honestly.

93
00:07:24.417 --> 00:07:27.127
See you in Unit twenty-eight. Goodbye!

94
00:07:27.427 --> 00:07:29.998
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

